Contents ( hide ) 1 Geography 2 Climate and glaciers 3 History 3.1 First ascent 3.2 Later ascents 3.3 First ascent from east side 4 Maps 5 See also 6 References 7 External links Geography ( edit ) The Vinson Massif extends between Goodge Col and Branscomb Glacier to the northwest , Nimitz Glacier and Gildea Glacier to the southwest and south , and Dater Glacier and its tributary Hinkley Glacier to the east . The southeastern part of the massif ends at Hammer Col , which joins it to the Craddock Massif , of which the highest point is Mount Rutford ( 4,477 metres ( 14,688 ft ) ) . The massif comprises both the high central Vinson Plateau with its few peaks rising to over 4,700 metres ( 15,400 ft ) , and several side ridges mostly trending southwest or northeast from the plateau .","entities":["Goodge Col","Nimitz Glacier","Gildea Glacier","Dater Glacier","Hinkley Glacier","Hammer Col","Craddock Massif","Mount Rutford","side ridges","Branscomb Glacier","Vinson Plateau","Vinson Massif"],"facts":["Vinson Massif -RELATED_TO-> Ultra","Vinson Massif -RELATED_TO-> Sentinel Range","Craddock Massif and Vinson Massif remeasured -RELATED_TO-> Craddock Massif","Roché Glacier -RELATED_TO-> Branscomb Glacier","Vinson Massif -RELATED_TO-> Ronne Ice Shelf"]}
