The Tropical Maritime Airmass is a warm humid and unstable trade wind due to its warmth . Convectional currents are easily set up within the airmass whenever there is little instability in the airmass as a result of a slight to a very high orographic uplift in mountainous regions like the obudu plateau or the heating of the land which can trigger the formation of cumulonimbus cloud leading to thunderstorms within the airmass . During the dominance of the Tropical Maritime Airmass ( MT ) in the rainy season of Nigeria , mornings are bright and sunny , the sun 's heating of the land in the mornings and afternoons sets up convectional currents , these currents rise vertically and cumulonimbus clouds are formed , by afternoons to evenings , torrential downpour follows . The Easterly wave or the African easterly waves is another major contributor of rainfall during the summer monsoons months of May to September in Nigeria . The nature of this waves changes at about the 15 degrees line . The waves that pass south of this line carry moisture and create convection that leads to rain clouds .","entities":["rainfall","Tropical Maritime airmass","convectional currents","trade wind","Obudu Plateau","cumulonimbus cloud","thunderstorms","Easterly wave","summer monsoons","15 degrees line","moisture","rain clouds","torrential downpour","heating of the land","orographic uplift"],"facts":["rainfall -RELATED_TO-> central Nigeria","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> dry season","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> River Niger Benue trough","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> south western escarpment of the Jos Plateau","rainfall -RELATED_TO-> condensation"]}
