Vibe explains that `` Eliza Hamilton steers the song as she reflects on her life , her marriage to Hamilton and their legacy , which includes the opening of New York City 's first private orphanage '' . Analysis ( edit ) The Atlantic 's review of the musical notes that Hamilton is n't remembered as fondly as other Founding Fathers ; Angelica sings `` Every other Founding Father story gets told . Every other Founding Father gets to grow old '' . The review also mentions that at the time of Hamilton 's extramarital - affair scandal , his political rival Thomas Jefferson was having a relationship with his slave which would have been a bigger scandal at the time , had it been known . During the entire musical , Hamilton sings about not wanting to throw away his shot and his wish to be placed firmly in the history books . Yet in his final moment , he decides to throw away his `` shot '' by aiming at the sky instead of at his duel opponent Aaron Burr . The final song speaks of his legacy that is in the end out of his control . The worth of a human being is decided after their death by people and events they have no agency over .","entities":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story","Aaron Burr","Thomas Jefferson","Angelica","Eliza Hamilton","Hamilton","Vibe","The Atlantic","the musical","his slave","Hamilton's extramarital-affair scandal","the duel","opening of New York City's first private orphanage","New York City's first private orphanage","New York City"],"facts":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Cast of Hamilton","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> show tune","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> English","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Founding Fathers","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> freedom"]}
{"title":"Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story","text":"Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story
So despite Hamilton 's best efforts to be remembered for certain things , history may see it a different way . Critical reception ( edit ) The Village Voice argues that this song 's refrain `` suggest ( s ) that we might look differently at the Founding Fathers and their ideas of freedom and equality , depending on who 's depicting them '' . NewYorkCityTheatre wrote that `` Eliza Hamilton sums up the story and leads the company to a moving finale , when the lights drop as the weight and vision of the American Dream continues its tug of war , between the immigrants who once founded this country and those looking to make her home '' . Variety wrote that `` in the end , Miranda 's impassioned narrative of one man 's story becomes the collective narrative of a nation , a nation built by immigrants who occasionally need to be reminded where they came from . '' Newsday described the finale as `` focused '' , while Uloop said it `` gives the heaviest meaning to any show in recent memory '' . The Huffington Post wrote `` The show ends with a bang , then a whimper , then a harmony . ''","entities":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story","Eliza Hamilton","Hamilton","Miranda","The Village Voice","NewYorkCityTheatre","Variety","Newsday","Uloop","The Huffington Post","Founding Fathers","freedom","equality","American Dream","immigrants"],"facts":["Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Cast of Hamilton","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> show tune","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> legacy","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> English","Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story -RELATED_TO-> Lin-Manuel Miranda"]}
