Legal cases ( edit ) The U.S. Supreme Court , in the case of Garcetti v. Ceballos , 04 - 473 , ruled in 2006 that government employees do not have protection from retaliation by their employers under the First Amendment of the Constitution when they speak pursuant to their official job duties . The U.S. Merit Systems Protection Board ( MSPB ) uses agency lawyers in the place of administrative law judges to decide federal employees ' whistleblower appeals . These lawyers , dubbed `` attorney examiners , '' deny 98 % of whistleblower appeals ; the Board and the Federal Circuit Court of Appeals give great deference to their initial decisions , resulting in affirmance rates of 97 % and 98 % , respectively .","entities":["U.S. Supreme Court","Garcetti v. Ceballos","government employees","employers","retaliation","First Amendment","Constitution","U.S. Merit Systems Protection Board","federal employees","federal employees' whistleblower appeals","Federal Circuit Court of Appeals","Whistleblower Protection Act"],"facts":["Whistleblower Protection Act -RELATED_TO-> Federal crime","Whistleblower Protection Act -RELATED_TO-> Immunity from prosecution","Whistleblower Protection Act -RELATED_TO-> Whistleblower protection in United States","Whistleblower Protection Act -RELATED_TO-> False Claims Act","Whistleblower Protection Act -RELATED_TO-> Informant"]}
