On the anterior surface of the petrous portion of the temporal bone are seen the eminence caused by the projection of the superior semicircular canal ; in front of and a little lateral to this a depression corresponding to the roof of the tympanic cavity ; the groove leading to the hiatus of the facial canal , for the transmission of the greater superficial petrosal nerve and the petrosal branch of the middle meningeal artery ; beneath it , the smaller groove , for the passage of the lesser superficial petrosal nerve ; and , near the apex of the bone , the depression for the semilunar ganglion and the orifice of the carotid canal . See also ( edit ) Anterior cranial fossa Posterior cranial fossa References ( edit ) This article incorporates text in the public domain from the 20th edition of Gray 's Anatomy ( 1918 ) Jump up ^ Mancall , Elliott L. ; Brock , David G. , eds. ( 2011 ) . `` Cranial Fossae '' . Gray 's Clinical Anatomy . Elsevier Health Sciences . p. 154 . ISBN 9781437735802 .","entities":["temporal bone","Middle cranial fossa","petrous portion of the temporal bone","anterior surface of the petrous portion","eminence","superior semicircular canal","depression corresponding to the roof of the tympanic cavity","tympanic cavity","groove leading to the hiatus of the facial canal","hiatus of the facial canal","greater superficial petrosal nerve","petrosal branch of the middle meningeal artery","smaller groove","lesser superficial petrosal nerve","apex of the bone"],"facts":["Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> Gray's Anatomy","Dr Jack M Kartush -RELATED_TO-> temporal bone","internal auditory canal -PART_OF-> temporal bone","Foramen ovale -RELATED_TO-> lesser superficial petrosal nerve","Facial canal -RELATED_TO-> CN-VII"]}
