Offshore spills , which are usually much greater in scale , contaminate coastal environments and cause a decline in local fishing production . Nigerian regulations are weak and rarely enforced allowing oil companies , in essence , to self - regulate . Natural gas flaring ( edit ) See also main article : Environmental issues in the Niger Delta Nigeria flares more natural gas associated with oil extraction than any other country , with estimates suggesting that of the 99,000,000 m ( 3.5 × 10 ^ cu ft ) of associated gas ( AG ) produced annually , 71,000,000 m ( 2.5 × 10 ^ cu ft ) , or about 70 % is wasted via flaring . Statistical data associated with gas flaring is notoriously unreliable , but AG wasted during flaring is estimated to cost Nigeria US $2.5 billion on a yearly basis . Companies operating in Nigeria harvest natural gas for commercial purposes , however prefer to extract it gas from deposits where it is found in isolation as non-associated gas . It is costly to separate commercially viable associated gas from oil , hence gas flaring to increase crude production .","entities":["natural gas","petroleum industry","oil companies","Niger Delta","Offshore spills","Coastal environments","Local fishing production","Nigerian regulations","Natural gas flaring","Environmental issues in the Niger Delta","Companies operating in Nigeria","Oil extraction","Associated gas","Non-associated gas","Gas deposits"],"facts":["West African Gas Pipeline -TRANSPORTS-> natural gas","Escravos Gas Utilization project -RELATED_TO-> natural gas","Nigerian Liquified Natural Gas Company -RELATED_TO-> natural gas","Gas Master Plan -PROMOTES-> natural gas","petroleum industry -RELATED_TO-> government external revenue"]}
