Freshwater and saltwater pearls ( edit ) Freshwater and saltwater pearls may sometimes look quite similar , but they come from different sources . Freshwater pearls form in various species of freshwater mussels , family Unionidae , which live in lakes , rivers , ponds and other bodies of fresh water . These freshwater pearl mussels occur not only in hotter climates , but also in colder more temperate areas such as Scotland ( where they are protected under law ) . Most freshwater cultured pearls sold today come from China . Saltwater pearls grow within pearl oysters , family Pteriidae , which live in oceans . Saltwater pearl oysters are usually cultivated in protected lagoons or volcanic atolls . Creation ( edit ) Diagram comparing a cross-section of a cultured pearl , upper , with a natural pearl , lower Pearls are formed inside the shell of certain mollusks as a defense mechanism against a potentially threatening irritant such as a parasite inside the shell , or an attack from outside that injures the mantle tissue . The mollusk creates a pearl sac to seal off the irritation .","entities":["Pearl","Pteriidae","Unionidae","Freshwater pearls","Saltwater pearls","Freshwater cultured pearls","Natural pearl","Freshwater mussels","Freshwater pearl mussels","Scotland","Pearl oysters","Oceans","Protected lagoons","Volcanic atolls","Mollusks"],"facts":["Pearl -RELATED_TO-> protein","Pearl -RELATED_TO-> living shelled mollusk","Pearl -RELATED_TO-> conulariid","Pearl -RELATED_TO-> soft tissue","Pearl -RELATED_TO-> Carbonate mineral"]}
