While white , and more recently black , saltwater pearls are by far the most popular , other color tints can be found on pearls from the oceans . Pink , blue , champagne , green , black and even purple saltwater pearls can be encountered , but to collect enough of these rare colors to form a complete string of the same size and same shade can take years . Religious references ( edit ) This section needs additional citations for verification . Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources . Unsourced material may be challenged and removed . ( March 2018 ) ( Learn how and when to remove this template message ) Hindu scriptures ( edit ) The Hindu tradition describes the sacred Nine Pearls which were first documented in the Garuda Purana , one of the books of the Hindu mythology . Ayurveda contains references to pearl powder as a stimulant of digestion and to treat mental ailments . According to Marco Polo , the kings of Malabar wore a necklace of 104 rubies and pearls which was given from one generation of kings to the next . The reason was that every king had to say 104 prayers every morning and every evening .","entities":["Pearls","Hindu tradition","Nine Pearls","Garuda Purana","Hindu mythology","Ayurveda","Marco Polo","Kings of Malabar","Malabar","Necklace of 104 rubies and pearls","Prayers","Pearl powder"],"facts":["Pearls -RELATED_TO-> Devanampiya Tissa's embassy","Pearl -RELATED_TO-> Pearls","Bern Physiologus -RELATED_TO-> Pearls","Britain -RELATED_TO-> Pearls","Pearls -RELATED_TO-> Prince Vijaya's embassy"]}
